.\"	@(#)mkstr.1	7.0 (2.11BSD) 2026/6/30
.\"
.TH MKSTR 1 "August 30, 2026"
.UC
.SH NAME
mkstr \- create message file from C source
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mkstr
[\fB-d\fP]
[\fB-l\fP]
[\fB-e\fP func] ...
[\fB-\fP]
strfile prefix file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mkstr
is used to create files of messages.  Usually these are error messages but may
be any string literal used as the first argument to a function.
Its use can save much D space in programs with large numbers of 
error, help, diagnostic strings.
.PP
.B mkstr
processes each \fIfile\fP, adding found strings to \fIstrfile\fP,
and placing a modified version of the input file in an output file with 
a name formed by catenating \fIprefix\fP and \fIfile\fP.
.PP
Options:
.TP
.B -d
Enable debug output. This displays the function names, append mode,
offset mode and files that would be processed.  No files are modified 
or created.
.TP
.B -l
Enable long offset mode.  The \fIlseek\fP offset that replaces the string
string literal is a \fBint\fP by default.  Use of \fB-l\fP generates
a \fBlong\fP offset.  Thus "error(91);" becomes "error(91L);".  This allows
a very large string file.  \fBbattlestar\fP(6) used this technique to create
its string file.
.TP
.B -e
name
adds a function name to processed for a string argument.  The trailing '('
should not be given.  Use of \fB-e\fP 
overrides the default \fBerror(\fP.  If \fBerror(\fP is to be matched add
an explicit \fB-e error\fP.
.TP
.B -
causes the strings to be appended to \fIstrfile\fP
This is used when recompiling parts of a program with multiple files.
.PP
.B mkstr
looks for the start of a string ('\fB"\fP') and if found does
a search for the names given by \fB-e\fP or the default \fBerror\fP.
If a match is found the C string (characters between opening and 
closing '\fB"\fP' including the null) is placed
in \fIstrfile\fP followed by a new-line character.
The new-line character makes it possible to sensibly
.B cat
\fIstrfile\fP
.sp
The modified copy of the input file contains in place of the string a
.I lseek
offset (either int or long depending on the \fB-l\fP flag) into the 
file which is used to retrieve the string.
.SH EXAMPLES
The examples are intentionally simplistic.  There are numerous programs
in the system sources that use \fBmkstr\fP that can be consulted.
.sp
A sample program tst1.c:
.IP
.nf
#include <stdio.h>
foobar(char *str) {puts(str);}
bazfoo(char *str) {puts(str);}
error(char *str)  {puts(str);}

main() 
	{ foobar("abc"); error("def"); bazfoo("ghi"); }
.fi
.PP
Typical usage:
.IP
	mkstr stringfile x tst1.c
.PP
This command would cause the characters \fBdef\\0\\n\fP
to be placed in the file \fIstringfile\fP,
and the line in the output file xtst1.c changed to:
.IP
foobar("abc"); error(0); bazfoo("ghi");
.PP
The function \fBerror(\fP is changed to use the string file:
.IP
.nf
.sp
error(a1)
	int	a1;
	{
	int	efil;
	char buf[256];

	efil = open("stringfile", 0);
	lseek(efil, (long) a1, 0);
	read(efil, buf, 256);
	puts(buf);
	close(efil);
	}
.fi
.sp
If the \fB-l\fP option was used when creating \fIstringfile\fP then the
function would change to:
.IP
.nf
error(a1)
	off_t	a1;
	{
	int	efil;
	char buf[256];

	efil = open("stringfile", 0);
	lseek(efil, a1, 0);
	read(efil, buf, 256)
	puts(buf);
	close(efil);
	}
.fi
.PP
To extract strings from functions in addition to \fBerror("\fP:
.IP
.nf
main() 
	{
	foobar("abc"); error("def"); bazfoo("ghi");
	}
.fi
.PP
Use \fB-e\fP thus:
.IP
.B mkstr -e
foobar
.B -e
error
.B -e
bazfoo stringfile x tst1.c
.PP
Of course the functions \fBfoobar(\fP and \fBbazfoo(\fP need to be
updated to use the string file.
.SH NOTES
The function name, its opening paren and the double quote must be adjacent:
\fBerror("\fP
NOT
\fBerror ("\fP
OR
\fBerror( "\fP
.PP
Previous versions of \fBmkstr\fP only looked for the 
function \fBerror("...")\fP.  The current version of \fBmkstr\fP 
allows 16 functions to be specified with \fB-e\fP.  The functions must 
be, of course, modified to open/lseek/read the string file as shown in the
example.
.SH BUGS
The parse is simplistic.  The default usage (no \fP-e\fP) means \fBerror(\fP
will match:
.sp
\fBstrsrerror("foo");\fP
.sp
This may not be expected but there are scripts in the system 
that depend on this behaviour.  Also
.sp
\fBcp = "error(";\fP
.sp
will match and produce corrupt output that will fail to compile.
.PP
The \fB-e\fP arguments are sorted by string length from longest to shortest.
.PP
It is now possible to match
\fBstrsrerror("foo");\fP
without matching \fBerror("foo");\fP by using \fB-e\fP:
.sp
\fBmkstr \fB-e\fP strsrerror ...
.sp
.PP
Continuation lines without a trailing backslash are rejected by the C
compiler but were accepted by the previous version of \fBmkstr\fP.  A
new-line was inserted into the string file at the break in the string.
This version of \fBmkstr\fP preserves that 
behaviour for backward compatibilty.  Proper continuation lines will not have 
the new-line inserted.
.IP
error("abc
.br
def");
.sp
produces: abc\\ndef);\\0\\n
.sp
error("abc\\
.br
def);
.sp
produces: abcdef);\\0\\n
.SH SEE ALSO
open(2), lseek(2), fgetln(3), read(2)
